title: Why user commands are JavaScript
tags: [[Customising the GUI]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: commands.js is code rather than a TOML table of commands; a user command is a builtin registered at runtime; a broken file stops the GUI instead of being skipped.

!! JavaScript, not TOML

A TOML table was considered first for user commands: a name, a template of the
invocation to run, the source of each argument's completions. The objection to
JavaScript was that the configuration repository would then contain code.

It already does: the panel types are JavaScript imported into the shell's page, and
the shipped scripts are shell. The TOML form, on the other hand, needed a small
language of its own to say where completions come from — a list, a query, the paths
of a field — to be invented, documented and then extended at each new need. A
function that returns the candidates needs none of it, and gets the same API panels
have ([[User commands]]).

The key of the export ''is'' the name: there is no `register` call that the file
could forget or do twice, and reloading knows exactly what the file defined.

!! A builtin registered at runtime

A user command has no owner in the command registry (`owner: None`), like the
shell's own commands: its code runs in the shell's page and in no panel. Two things
follow.

* Its `mf` is built for the ''focused workspace'', and the workspace is read through a getter at each call, not captured when the file is loaded.
* The dispatch looks for it after the shell's own `switch` and before the panels' commands, and the registry refuses to register it under the name of a shell command (`CommandRegistry::register_user`). Before that refusal the entry's label and argument declaration replaced the builtin's while the builtin still ran, and a reload removed the builtin from the registry.

`mf.invoke` exists because of this feature. The API only had `commands.invoke`,
while everything written about user commands said `mf.invoke`: combining commands is
the whole point, so the call is at the top of the object, for panels too.

!! A broken file stops the GUI

A missing file is caught by Rust before any window opens, like any other missing
configuration file. A file that does not parse can only be seen by the web view —
Rust has no JavaScript engine. The shell therefore imports it during its own start,
''outside'' the error boundary that isolates a panel's module, and a rejection leaves
the initialisation banner on screen with the error. No panel is mounted, so no panel
command exists either. The scripting API still answers for what Rust serves alone
(the layout), because its listener is installed before the import.

Skipping a broken file would start a GUI in which keys silently run nothing.

On a reload the order matters: the module is imported and validated ''first'', and
the previous commands are forgotten only then. Clearing first meant that one bad
edit removed every user command until the file parsed again.

!! Testing it

Both bugs above the unit tests could not see were found by driving the real GUI:
start it on another port, then send invocations to it:

```
mf gui --gui-url http://127.0.0.1:7534 command "<invocation>"
```

`/gui/command` goes through the shell's dispatch, so an answer
proves the shell runs, and `unknown command` on a panel's command proves it does
not.
